<Base title="FPGA routes"><header class="page-title"><p class="eyebrow">ADIUVO FORGIX / PCIE CANDIDATE / S3 REFERENCE</p><h1>Put the FPGA where<br />the data actually reaches it.</h1><p class="lede">An FPGA can count, buffer, filter or move samples. It cannot automatically remove the original ESP32's modem access and SRAM limits.</p><a href={url("work/?work=the-fpga-route-has-a-measured-feasibility-decision")}>Open the FPGA feasibility proposal →</a></header>
<div class="card-grid"><article class="research-card"><p class="eyebrow">YOUR SMALL FPGA / USER-REPORTED</p><h3>Adiuvo Forgix</h3><p>The official design pairs an Efinix Trion T8F49 (7,384 logic elements) with an RP2354, PSRAM and 3.3 V I/O. USB is USB 1.1 through the RP chip. Verify the actual revision and available wiring before using it.</p><p><strong>Useful first jobs:</strong> synthetic sequence counters, FIFO and trigger exercises, clock measurements, bounded SPI transfer, and decimated or spectral output.</p><p>It is not the FT600 USB 3 + DDR architecture used by eSpDR. Raw 80 MS/s × 20 bits is 200 MB/s before framing.</p><a href="https://forgix.tech/">Official Forgix design ↗</a></article><article class="research-card"><p class="eyebrow">HOST INVENTORY / PARTLY IDENTIFIED</p><h3>PCIe FPGA candidate</h3><p>This host has a PCIe device with ID dabc:1017. Neighboring research notes associate that identity with an Alibaba AS02MC04 and a Kintex UltraScale+ XCKU3P. The exact board and bitstream are not independently established by this probe.</p><p><strong>Potential jobs:</strong> deeper DSP, buffering and a host DMA path after independent JTAG, clock, memory and PCIe bring-up.</p><p>Existing notes report no external DRAM. Driver state, usable memory, connector pinout and sustained DMA remain open gates.</p><a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/fpga-inventory/README.md")}>Inventory and provenance →</a></article><article class="research-card"><p class="eyebrow">UPSTREAM REFERENCE / DIFFERENT ESP CHIP</p><h3>eSpDR on ESP32-S3</h3><p>The demonstrated route uses S3 dedicated GPIO and SIMD, an Artix-7 with DDR and FT600 USB 3. It is a reference for a separate front end, not a firmware image for our LX6 board.</p><p>Current documentation keeps the ESP's native 40 MHz crystal and forwards an ESP-generated clock to the FPGA. The article's clock description is older.</p><a href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/eSpDR/tree/41a0ffe279119df942e4a9e892dc19d196e1c952">Pinned reference design ↗</a></article></div>
<section class="section split"><article class="note-box"><h3>First gate: a synthetic transport</h3><p>Inventory the exact board, voltage, pins, clocks and host path. Move numbered synthetic packets with CRCs. Record rate, losses, stalls and backlog over time before connecting the radio path.</p></article><article class="note-box"><h3>Second gate: original ESP32 data access</h3><p>Measure a bounded SPI/I2S or snapshot route on LX6. If capture is still discontinuous, publish that limit. A lower-rate feature extractor may be useful even when raw streaming is infeasible.</p></article></section>
<section class="section prose-note"><p>Sources: <a href="https://forgix.tech/">Adiuvo's Forgix specifications</a>, <a href="https://www.efinixinc.com/blog/blog-2026-how-efinix-trion-powers-the-forgix-board.html">Efinix board overview</a> and <a href="https://github.com/enjoy-digital/aduivo_forgix_test">primary LiteX/SPIBone demonstration</a>. The demonstration establishes a possible development route; its firmware and pin mapping have not been verified on your board.</p></section></Base>
